WEBVTT

1
00:58:16.640 --> 00:58:18.720
So I'm an independent researcher.

2
00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:22.760
My interests are in sort
of theoretical approaches to intersections

3
00:58:22.760 --> 00:58:26.200
of art and technology, generally speaking,
and in continental philosophy.

4
00:58:26.360 --> 00:58:29.360
But the paper today
is about electronic literature

5
00:58:29.360 --> 00:58:32.480
and AI data work,
and it is based on my personal experience

6
00:58:32.480 --> 00:58:35.800
of working as a transcriber
for AI projects

7
00:58:36.480 --> 00:58:39.480
between 2023 and 2025,

8
00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:44.120
in a business process outsourcing company
located in Eastern Europe.

9
00:58:44.480 --> 00:58:48.560
So in the form that I experienced
it, transcription for AI projects

10
00:58:48.560 --> 00:58:52.080
consisted of listening to audio recordings
provided by the client,

11
00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:54.680
which is a major tech company
whose name cannot be mentioned

12
00:58:54.680 --> 00:58:58.760
because of non-disclosure agreements,
and either transcribing those audio

13
00:58:58.760 --> 00:59:03.320
recordings from scratch or correcting
an AI generated transcription.

14
00:59:03.400 --> 00:59:06.600
The recordings are gathered
from users of digital devices,

15
00:59:06.600 --> 00:59:10.560
many of whom appear to be unaware
of actually being recorded.

16
00:59:11.360 --> 00:59:15.160
So my argument today
is that the stream of audio recordings

17
00:59:15.160 --> 00:59:18.560
constitutes an impressive piece
of electronic literature,

18
00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:22.040
despite the fact that it is not intended
as such.

19
00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:26.160
An extraordinary cut up literary
composition of uncreative writing

20
00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:29.720
made up of fragments of daily life,
often caught unawares,

21
00:59:30.040 --> 00:59:33.560
combined with moments of musique concrète
from background noises to glitches

22
00:59:33.560 --> 00:59:35.360
in the recording process, and so on.

23
00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:37.400
I propose that the

24
00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:41.280
event that makes the difference between
the audio recordings as a raw material

25
00:59:41.280 --> 00:59:44.840
exploited in the industrial production
of so-called artificial intelligence

26
00:59:45.040 --> 00:59:47.920
and the same audio recordings
as a piece of literature,

27
00:59:47.920 --> 00:59:52.600
is the creation by transcribers
of what I call imaginary artworks.

28
00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:54.880
And I'll tell you a bit
more what I mean by that.

29
00:59:54.880 --> 00:59:57.840
So this paper aims to provide
the starting point for understanding

30
00:59:57.840 --> 01:00:00.920
this concealed,
unintended piece of electronic literature

31
01:00:00.920 --> 01:00:05.560
and the imaginary artworks
that I claim are associated with it.

32
01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:09.200
Now, because of the imperative

33
01:00:09.200 --> 01:00:12.200
of non-disclosure
that governs AI data work.

34
01:00:13.440 --> 01:00:16.440
I cannot reveal any exact details
about the context

35
01:00:16.440 --> 01:00:20.600
of the transcription work,
nor about the audio recordings themselves.

36
01:00:20.680 --> 01:00:24.560
And I take this prohibition
to be actually a very inciting

37
01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:27.560
and fertile ground
for the topic of this paper.

38
01:00:28.120 --> 01:00:30.880
In fact, all of you already know enough
about the subject

39
01:00:30.880 --> 01:00:34.040
in order to be able to glitch
this logic of enclosure

40
01:00:34.080 --> 01:00:37.320
through some very simple
exercises of imagination.

41
01:00:38.240 --> 01:00:39.960
What was I transcribing?

42
01:00:39.960 --> 01:00:44.080
Well, everything that you can imagine
a user saying towards a device with voice

43
01:00:44.080 --> 01:00:47.360
recognition capabilities,
or more exactly in the presence

44
01:00:47.360 --> 01:00:49.920
of such a device, given
the fact that recording can be

45
01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:52.840
unintentionally triggered.

46
01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:55.640
And you know this
because of whistleblower articles

47
01:00:55.640 --> 01:00:57.480
that were already published in the press.

48
01:00:57.480 --> 01:01:00.720
And these are some examples from 2019
that I'm aware of,

49
01:01:00.760 --> 01:01:03.760
but there are probably many others since.

50
01:01:03.800 --> 01:01:07.520
And you also know it
because you are presumably agreeing

51
01:01:07.560 --> 01:01:11.720
to this in the terms and conditions
of your applications on your smartphones.

52
01:01:12.200 --> 01:01:16.040
Given the fact that you can be recorded
just as well by your own devices

53
01:01:16.080 --> 01:01:19.680
as by the devices of other users,
it is only a small logical step

54
01:01:19.680 --> 01:01:23.640
to realize that because of the general
uptake in our societies

55
01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:27.600
of such digital technologies
with voice recognition capabilities,

56
01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:32.880
you could be recorded at any moment,
intentionally or not, by

57
01:01:32.880 --> 01:01:35.920
the device is owned by your neighbor,
by the policeman harassing you,

58
01:01:35.920 --> 01:01:39.640
by the doctor's office, the company
that provides your medical supplies,

59
01:01:39.720 --> 01:01:43.560
your lawyer, the phone company,
the supermarket tech support company,

60
01:01:43.560 --> 01:01:44.480
and so on.

61
01:01:44.480 --> 01:01:49.040
And the sample of all of this does feed
into the electronic piece of uncreative

62
01:01:49.040 --> 01:01:54.760
writing that transcribers are witnessing
on a daily basis behind closed doors.

63
01:01:55.720 --> 01:01:57.920
Now, in terms of format,

64
01:01:57.920 --> 01:02:01.720
the length of the audio recordings ranges
from all the way from a few seconds

65
01:02:01.720 --> 01:02:04.840
long, and such recordings are transcribed
in their hundreds or thousands per day

66
01:02:04.840 --> 01:02:08.040
by each transcriber to instances
that are over an hour long,

67
01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:11.680
which can require multiple work days
on the same audio recording.

68
01:02:12.080 --> 01:02:15.240
The order of the recordings
seems to be random from the transcriber’s

69
01:02:15.240 --> 01:02:18.920
perspective, but they are grouped
in so-called projects that give them

70
01:02:18.920 --> 01:02:23.000
a certain degree of consistency,
both in terms of format and content.

71
01:02:23.920 --> 01:02:27.120
Moreover, many recordings
appear in the data set multiple times.

72
01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:28.120
Presumably, this happens

73
01:02:28.120 --> 01:02:31.680
in order to ensure consistency across
multiple transcriptions.

74
01:02:32.040 --> 01:02:36.040
Hence, for the transcriber,
an approximate interwoven

75
01:02:36.080 --> 01:02:39.080
textual structure is slowly contoured,

76
01:02:39.200 --> 01:02:42.040
punctuated by repetitions and callbacks,

77
01:02:42.040 --> 01:02:45.840
a loose structure that encourages
a creative spectator engagement.

78
01:02:46.040 --> 01:02:51.200
Despite oneself, one starts building up
meaningful if imaginary connections,

79
01:02:51.240 --> 01:02:55.720
narrative, poetic, musical,
or otherwise, between the recordings

80
01:02:56.520 --> 01:02:59.600
during the transcription work,
probably due to the excruciating boredom

81
01:02:59.600 --> 01:03:03.960
of just performing the same robotic tasks
again and again, imagination

82
01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:07.040
tends to take over
you want it or not, and combines

83
01:03:07.040 --> 01:03:08.880
the recordings between them
and with fragments

84
01:03:08.880 --> 01:03:13.520
of personal experience and memories
into these complex, surreal compositions.

85
01:03:13.560 --> 01:03:16.720
Audio, visual, haptic and so on.

86
01:03:17.040 --> 01:03:20.320
Inspired by the imaginary music
of composer Octavian Nemescu,

87
01:03:21.080 --> 01:03:23.960
I suggest understanding
such imaginary compositions

88
01:03:23.960 --> 01:03:28.160
that exist only for a very brief moment
in the imagination of transcribers

89
01:03:28.640 --> 01:03:32.040
as artworks in their own right,
despite the fact that they remain

90
01:03:32.040 --> 01:03:35.400
singular, personal,
unshakable experiences.

91
01:03:36.240 --> 01:03:39.480
So let me give you a bit of an idea
of what imaginary music is in order to

92
01:03:39.520 --> 01:03:41.640
understand what I'm driving it.

93
01:03:41.640 --> 01:03:46.880
Nemescu started working with this idea
of imaginary music around 1974, 1975,

94
01:03:47.000 --> 01:03:51.240
and for him, it is a musical genre
characterized by the fact that it is

95
01:03:51.240 --> 01:03:52.880
radically anti spectacular,

96
01:03:52.880 --> 01:03:56.160
being performed by each spectator
in their own imagination.

97
01:03:56.320 --> 01:04:00.040
A piece of imaginary music
is a guided exercise of imagination

98
01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:03.320
that starts with a score written
by the composer, oftentimes

99
01:04:03.320 --> 01:04:05.640
using experimental
and conventional notation,

100
01:04:05.640 --> 01:04:08.640
and you have fragments of such a score
on the screen.

101
01:04:08.960 --> 01:04:12.960
But it is then the task of the spectator
to study to the best of their ability,

102
01:04:13.000 --> 01:04:15.280
the score, to rehearse it actually,

103
01:04:15.280 --> 01:04:19.400
and to perform it in their imagination
as best as they can.

104
01:04:19.640 --> 01:04:22.200
There is no exterior trace
of the performance.

105
01:04:22.200 --> 01:04:27.440
For Nemescu, imaginary music constitutes
an interior ritual that destabilizes

106
01:04:27.640 --> 01:04:31.520
societal paradigms
based on the spectacle, and proposes

107
01:04:31.520 --> 01:04:35.360
new ways for the individual to connect
and engage with their environments.

108
01:04:35.960 --> 01:04:39.520
So I advocate for thinking
about the imaginary compositions

109
01:04:39.520 --> 01:04:44.040
that emerge for transcribers
during the labor process as artworks

110
01:04:44.040 --> 01:04:48.520
similar to the pieces of imaginary music
performed by spectators.

111
01:04:49.080 --> 01:04:49.920
The starting point,

112
01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:53.960
though, for these artworks, is not a score
written by a composer or an artist,

113
01:04:53.960 --> 01:04:59.080
but this stream of audio recordings
of everyday life caught unawares.

114
01:04:59.920 --> 01:05:03.120
So imagine, for example, a project
that is an extensive

115
01:05:03.120 --> 01:05:06.240
set of audio recordings
with a certain degree of consistency

116
01:05:06.760 --> 01:05:09.360
that transcribers will engage
with for multiple days in a row

117
01:05:09.360 --> 01:05:12.600
and sometimes weeks, often weeks
and sometimes even months.

118
01:05:12.640 --> 01:05:18.280
So imagine a project that contains, say,
10% recordings related to pornography

119
01:05:18.280 --> 01:05:23.680
and just as much related to prayers,
which in my experience is pretty usual.

120
01:05:24.800 --> 01:05:27.680
Some of these audio recordings
are spread over multiple audio

121
01:05:27.680 --> 01:05:31.320
tracks,
fragmented and repeated multiple times.

122
01:05:31.920 --> 01:05:36.840
This text texture, with its loose,
interwoven threads that cut across two

123
01:05:36.880 --> 01:05:40.760
highly contested areas of contemporary
cultures, pornography and prayers,

124
01:05:41.280 --> 01:05:43.360
offers a very fertile ground for sense

125
01:05:43.360 --> 01:05:47.040
making, driven
maybe by the power of the false.

126
01:05:47.560 --> 01:05:48.680
Whereby the power of the false,

127
01:05:48.680 --> 01:05:52.640
inspired by Deleuze’s concept,
I simply understand this proliferation

128
01:05:52.640 --> 01:05:57.520
of heterogeneous senses
upon the non-linear intermission of texts.

129
01:05:57.560 --> 01:06:00.640
This non-linear cut ups of daily life.

130
01:06:01.560 --> 01:06:04.920
Incidentally, such falsifications
or fabrications, I believe,

131
01:06:05.040 --> 01:06:07.960
seem to constitute
some of the most truthful representations

132
01:06:07.960 --> 01:06:11.280
of life and death
in contemporary digital cultures.

133
01:06:11.480 --> 01:06:15.120
No other artists that I'm aware of,
or literature piece for that matter,

134
01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:19.440
proves either the technological
and financial capacity or indeed

135
01:06:19.440 --> 01:06:25.200
the ethical irresponsibility to offer its
spectators such an overwhelming boring.

136
01:06:25.840 --> 01:06:28.880
Paradoxically,
at the same time, exhilarating patchwork

137
01:06:28.920 --> 01:06:32.040
of intimate moments of
daily life caught unawares,

138
01:06:34.680 --> 01:06:35.400
that are offered

139
01:06:35.400 --> 01:06:39.680
as the pieces of a fragmentary
and ambiguous puzzle that is continuously

140
01:06:39.680 --> 01:06:43.920
constructed and deconstructed
by the spectator’s/worker’s imagination.

141
01:06:45.320 --> 01:06:48.480
So these streams of content,
in my opinion,

142
01:06:48.520 --> 01:06:51.320
that AI data workers
face on a daily basis, constitute

143
01:06:51.320 --> 01:06:55.120
some of the most intriguing masterpieces
of contemporary art and literature

144
01:06:55.200 --> 01:06:58.640
in the second and third
decades of the 21st century,

145
01:06:58.640 --> 01:07:01.960
and the fact that they are available
only to AI data workers

146
01:07:01.960 --> 01:07:05.800
and inaccessible to art institutions
and their traditional publics,

147
01:07:05.800 --> 01:07:09.480
I think, should not stop us
from recognizing and valuing them as such.

148
01:07:10.000 --> 01:07:11.000
Now I'll get to my conclusion.

149
01:07:11.000 --> 01:07:12.040
But instead of a conclusion,

150
01:07:12.040 --> 01:07:16.120
I want to show you half a joke,
half serious, a score of sorts.

151
01:07:16.240 --> 01:07:17.760
This time for a piece of imaginary

152
01:07:17.760 --> 01:07:20.920
electronic literature
instead of a piece of imaginary music.

153
01:07:21.400 --> 01:07:24.840
And I invite each of you to perform it
whenever you want, in your own time,

154
01:07:24.840 --> 01:07:29.120
when you find it convenient, following
the instructions as closely as possible.

155
01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:30.640
Now there is a trigger warning.

156
01:07:30.640 --> 01:07:34.240
There is some mild explicit slang words
in the piece, and it does engage

157
01:07:34.240 --> 01:07:37.640
with themes of death and sexuality. So

158
01:07:38.840 --> 01:07:39.240
the piece

159
01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:42.240
is called “How to Say in Russian: ‘Hey
Kevin, you're a dick.’”

160
01:07:43.000 --> 01:07:46.720
It is to be performed
in a crowded public space of your choice.

161
01:07:46.960 --> 01:07:51.240
First movement recommended time
between 3 and 5 minutes.

162
01:07:51.680 --> 01:07:55.520
Remember instances of voice recording
software that you have used lately?

163
01:07:56.120 --> 01:07:59.000
For example, do you have any smart home
assistant devices?

164
01:07:59.000 --> 01:08:02.880
Do you use voice search on your smartphone
speech to text on your keyboard?

165
01:08:02.920 --> 01:08:05.360
Does your doctor have a phone
answering machine?

166
01:08:05.360 --> 01:08:06.680
What about your lawyer?

167
01:08:06.680 --> 01:08:09.600
Have you contacted any customer
service lines lately?

168
01:08:09.600 --> 01:08:10.880
Have you used any translation

169
01:08:10.880 --> 01:08:13.880
devices with voice recognition
on your smartphone, etc.?

170
01:08:13.960 --> 01:08:17.600
And try to remember some of the things
that you said in the presence

171
01:08:17.600 --> 01:08:21.120
of digital devices
with such voice recognition software.

172
01:08:22.040 --> 01:08:26.240
Second movement,
recommended time between 5 and 10 minutes.

173
01:08:26.960 --> 01:08:30.120
Imagine what your parents,
what your children, your friends,

174
01:08:30.120 --> 01:08:34.680
your neighbors, your colleagues say
in the presence of such devices.

175
01:08:34.960 --> 01:08:36.240
Don't be shy.

176
01:08:36.240 --> 01:08:38.240
Imagine what they are
whispering under the blankets

177
01:08:38.240 --> 01:08:40.800
and what they are
screaming in the public square.

178
01:08:40.800 --> 01:08:43.600
Imagine what they are saying
on the hospital bed, in the pub

179
01:08:43.600 --> 01:08:45.520
or in the classroom.

180
01:08:45.520 --> 01:08:47.520
Third movement.

181
01:08:47.520 --> 01:08:48.920
No time limit.

182
01:08:48.920 --> 01:08:50.880
I will finish in just a second.

183
01:08:50.880 --> 01:08:53.200
Look around
you and choose a random person.

184
01:08:53.200 --> 01:08:54.560
Imagine them being happy.

185
01:08:54.560 --> 01:08:57.120
Imagine them being confused and angry.

186
01:08:57.120 --> 01:08:58.400
Imagine them crying.

187
01:08:58.400 --> 01:09:01.200
Imagine them masturbating and having sex.

188
01:09:01.200 --> 01:09:02.840
Imagine them being excited.

189
01:09:02.840 --> 01:09:04.320
Imagine them being bored.

190
01:09:04.320 --> 01:09:06.320
Imagine them being scared.

191
01:09:06.320 --> 01:09:08.240
Imagine them suffering and dying.

192
01:09:08.240 --> 01:09:10.600
Imagine them in their tedious daily
routines.

193
01:09:10.600 --> 01:09:11.600
And imagine

194
01:09:11.600 --> 01:09:15.520
all the fragments that the digital devices
are capturing from all of this.

195
01:09:15.560 --> 01:09:16.720
Not only what is said,

196
01:09:16.720 --> 01:09:19.640
but the unintelligible sounds
and the acoustic backgrounds,

197
01:09:19.640 --> 01:09:21.120
the breathing in and out,

198
01:09:21.120 --> 01:09:24.960
the musicality of the voice,
the whispers, the screams, the coughing,

199
01:09:24.960 --> 01:09:28.440
spitting, grunting,
snoring, panting and what have you.

200
01:09:29.360 --> 01:09:33.160
Imagine the background TV noises, the car
engines, the rhythmic clicks

201
01:09:33.160 --> 01:09:35.000
and clocks of unknown devices,

202
01:09:35.000 --> 01:09:39.120
the computer games, shopping centers,
hospitals, the crowds, the voice

203
01:09:39.120 --> 01:09:44.200
robots, the toilets flushing, doors
opening and closing, songbirds, rain,

204
01:09:44.240 --> 01:09:49.200
traffic jams, and everything that
there are actually no words to describe.

205
01:09:49.720 --> 01:09:51.080
Fourth movement.

206
01:09:51.080 --> 01:09:53.240
Forget it all now.

207
01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:54.280
If you go through this piece,

208
01:09:54.280 --> 01:09:57.920
you will have a pretty good idea
of what transcribers are listening to

209
01:09:58.040 --> 01:10:01.040
when they are doing the work of audio
transcription for AI projects.

210
01:10:01.240 --> 01:10:04.800
I will finish here, but before stopping,
I just want 30 more seconds for a

211
01:10:04.800 --> 01:10:05.840
quick note.

212
01:10:05.840 --> 01:10:09.440
I find it very important to mention this
every time when I talk about this topic.

213
01:10:09.440 --> 01:10:12.800
In academic contexts,
there is a growing academic literature

214
01:10:12.800 --> 01:10:15.880
that insists on the exploitation
of cheap labor in AI data work.

215
01:10:15.880 --> 01:10:18.840
I'm sure that you are all aware of that
and in general lines.

216
01:10:18.840 --> 01:10:21.600
I subscribe to many of the arguments
made in this sense.

217
01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:24.080
In my experience, though,
and I don't want to generalize.

218
01:10:24.080 --> 01:10:27.200
I'm referring strictly to my experience,
and I want to mention this

219
01:10:27.240 --> 01:10:28.400
the exploitative practices

220
01:10:28.400 --> 01:10:31.920
that I encountered
while working as a transcriber in a video

221
01:10:32.440 --> 01:10:37.080
were definitely not as pronounced as those
that I encountered as a PhD researcher

222
01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:39.520
for four years
and as a sessional teaching staff

223
01:10:39.520 --> 01:10:42.360
for a couple of semesters
at a top university.

224
01:10:42.360 --> 01:10:45.440
And with that, I will finish. Thank you.
